  "notes": "TIMINGS: pulled from the workflow's event history (Python SDK fetch_history_events() after the workflow completed), not from wall-clock prints in the harness. created_at = ActivityTaskScheduled.event_time, started_at = ActivityTaskStarted.event_time, completed_at = ActivityTaskCompleted.event_time; Started/Completed are matched back to their Scheduled event via scheduled_event_id, entries are ordered by scheduled event id (== created_at order) and expressed in seconds relative to the first ActivityTaskScheduled. All three are server-assigned timestamps from the single Temporal server on this box, so one clock throughout.\nACTIVITYTASKSTARTED CAVEAT: the server does not persist ActivityTaskStarted when it dispatches the task; it keeps it in mutable state and flushes it to history together with the terminal event (ActivityTaskCompleted here). The event_time is still the real dispatch time, but it is only readable once the activity has finished, so the harness fetches the full history after the workflow completes rather than streaming events live. Only the final attempt's Started event survives a retry, so the harness asserts every activity is attempt 1 and that no ActivityTaskFailed/TimedOut or WorkflowTaskFailed/TimedOut event appears in any history; none did. started_at is when the server handed the task to the worker, not when the Python function body entered (~4ms of dispatch here); SDK-internal queueing inside that is not visible in history.\nSETUP: official temporalio/docker-compose, postgres variant (docker-compose-postgres.yml), temporalio/auto-setup:1.29.7 + postgres:16, stock dynamicconfig/development-sql.yaml, default namespace, 4 history shards. Server 1.30-1.32 exist but temporalio/auto-setup publishes no image past 1.29.x, so 1.29.7 is the newest server reachable through the official docker-compose path. One worker process, default worker tuning, task queue \"bench\". Activities are sync defs, so the worker is given activity_executor=ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=100) - the documented way to run sync/CPU-bound activities, sized to the default max_concurrent_activities=100. The workflow awaits each execute_activity before scheduling the next, so exactly one activity is ever in flight. Everything (server, postgres, worker, harness) runs on one m7i.xlarge (4 vCPU, 15GB, Amazon Linux 2023); CPU was ~98% idle during the runs.\nINTERPRETER: the \"interpreter\" field was measured by an extra activity run on the same worker process and the same ThreadPoolExecutor as the benchmark activities (fibo(33) three times then fibo(10) once, timed with time.perf_counter inside the activity), so it reflects the exact CPython build the activities execute on.\nSUPPLEMENTARY RUN, NOT THE DEFAULT CONFIG: identical to temporal_40_10.json except that history.transferProcessorMaxPollRPS was raised from its stock default of 20 to 1000 in dynamicconfig/development-sql.yaml, with >2 minutes allowed for every per-shard rate limiter to pick the new value up (they refresh on a ~1 minute timer, so runs taken sooner still show the old throttle on some shards). This isolates how much of the default-config result is Temporal's dev-scale task-dispatch rate limit rather than its orchestration cost: ~3.0s becomes ~0.75s, i.e. ~19ms per sequential activity. The dynamic config was reverted to stock after this run."
